            Assert.IsTrue(Old_index == New_arrListLastIndexOfX && Old_index == New_arrListIndexOfX);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);"  that @@@ new position of x in array is same as old position in array     
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX && Old_arrListIndexOfX == index);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);"  that  @@@  position of x in array after modification is same as old position
	    Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX == index);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);"  that  @@@ last index of x in array after modification is greater or = to old last index 
	    Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(A.Count\(.*?\) == B\.Count\(.*?\)\);"  that  @@@ that @@@ number of elements in old array is same in new array
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);"  that  @@@x still exists in array
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}
```

Note: There are examples of the following assertions:
Assert.IsTrue(Old_index == New_arrListLastIndexOfX && Old_index == New_arrListIndexOfX);
Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX && Old_arrListIndexOfX == index);
Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX == index);
Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);

This is an example of how you can use Pex in your tests with an Assert in Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX).

Assert.IsTrue - if provided condition is not met, Assert.Fail is invoked which causes the test to fail.

Pex method returns a state of the test under its control and doesn’t influence the test - it only provides information for the purpose of testing the code for properties of different execution paths etc.

Remember that you can also use PexTryCatch to try, catch expected exception patterns and perform your custom assertions inside the block.

Note: In order to use Assert, you need to set the Test Settings to check the "Enable code coverage on build" option under Test -> Test Settings -> (Debug or Release) -> General -> Code coverage.

Source link: [https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/using-microsoft-visualstudio-testtools-unittesting-test-order?view=vs-2019](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/using-microsoft-visualstudio-testtools-unittesting-test-order?view=vs-2019)

