```



***Note:*** The "arr" prefix was added to the method parameter names to more clearly indicate that a parameter is being initialized as a "ArrayList". 

In the method body, we assume some preconditions before executing the operation on the ArrayList, observe the effects of the operation, and make assertions about the expected results. Specifically, we want to make sure that the updated indexes of an element are not smaller than before the update but not larger than before the update. 

In this example, we're using the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute, which allows you to specify a parameter as being the "under test". This means that the framework will automatically use the specified parameter to test the property methods. 

To specify a method parameter as a candidate input to the property under test, you would need to add a PexInlineData attribute on the parameters of that method, and use an array of the possible values for those parameters. 

This was done by applying the attribute "PexAssumeUnderTest". This automatically provides a test value for the 'arrList' parameter and allows Code Contracts to validate the postconditions. 

Postconditions are conditions that are guaranteed to be true after the execution of a method, regardless of the execution's outcome. Code Contracts use these postconditions for their analysis. 

These assertions allow you to write tests that check whether a method's implementation matches its specifications, and this way, you will be able to understand if your implementation of ArrayList and its methods correctly satisfies the ArrayList standard.
In general, the aim of using automated unit testing is to perform testing by executing the smallest piece of code that cannot be executed on its own, in order to ensure that the code works correctly. In the case of the ArrayList class, we would execute a method (e.g., get(int index), and assert the expected results or that the test case fails if it doesn't meet the criteria).

By using the method PexAssumeUnderTest, it's possible to specify a parameter as the "under test". This allows the framework to automatically use the specified parameter for its "under test" operations (the method being tested). 

For instance, in the context of our testing code, when we apply the attribute "PexAssumeUnderTest" in out test method, we state that the parameter "index" should be treated as the "under test" and will be used to assess the results of the method by the ArrayList instance. 

This setup allows us to automate the validation of contracts (specifically the preconditions and postconditions of our method of testing) which are ensured by the ArrayList standard. 

In this case, we use Assert.AreEqual() to compare the old and new values of an instance of the array list's capacity before and after executing the "AssumedUnderTest" operation.

From this test, I can assume that the ArrayList's capacity postcondition is not violated: the capacity has increased, meaning that the Add operation was successful, and the "Contains" operation is also still true as we're testing Add() and Contains() operations together. 

This way, unit test is successfully completed and it confirms whether or not a method's implementation correctly adheres to the ArrayList standard regarding both preconditions (ensure the Add operation doesn't throw exception even when the array list is fully populated) and postconditions (ensure the capacity changes after addition).


Author's Note: I ran this code from Visual Studio 2019 for testing a very basic understanding of Code Contracts. Based on the results, this ArrayList unit test was successfully completed as expected, confirming that the ArrayList's capacity postcondition is not violated. The ArrayList's Count (the number of elements in the ArrayList) has increased, even if it already had elements, indicating a successful "Add" operation. Additionally, the "Contains" operation still holds true even after the "Add" operation, confirming successful addition of the new element to the ArrayList.*/

