Testing with [Pex](https://rehansaeed.com/pex-a-new-test-exercise-tool/) will execute a variety of input values, and show you the results in a user-friendly format. You can use the "PexObserve" to show the states of variables.

In the test method, we assume that the precondition for index is true because Pex will automatically handle all other paths that contradict the precondition. 

The goal of Pex is to exercise the "branches" in the code where index >= 0 and index < arrList.Count.

Additionally, Pex verifies the postconditions of the method after it has executed. Here, you verify that the old index of x is not greater than the new index.

This is a good practice because it generates a large amount of test cases, providing a more thorough testing of the code. It also allows you to verify that the assumptions (preconditions) that you made about the input parameters are satisfied.

Please note that the actual code might not have a method to test set functionality, as the ArrayList Contract Test would test a list of all methods through properties. But you can use the above approach to test any method (like set in our case).---
title: "C# - How to make a console application which can switch between multiple activities?"
date: 2020-06-10T11:28:32-07:00
draft: false
author: "Lam Thanh Lam"
description: "A step by step guide on how to make a basic console application that can switch between different activities using C#."
tags: ["C#", "Console Application", "Switch Statement"]
---
In this guide, I’m going to show you how to create a simple console application in C# that has multiple activities. Activities will be defined by a specific enumerator type. We will use a `switch` statement to switch between different behaviors representing the different activities.

Here are the broad steps we are going to follow:

1. Define an Enumerator Type that represents different activities
2. Create methods to handle each activity
3. Create the main function that will switch between different activities
4. Write switch logic

Let's start:

```csharp
using System;

namespace ActivityApp
{
    // Define an enumerator type that represents different activities
    enum ActivityType
    {
        Activity1 = 1,
        Activity2,
        Activity3,
        Exit
    }
    
    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            bool continueApp = true;

            while(continueApp)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Please choose an activity:\n" +
                    "1. Activity 1\n" +
                    "2. Activity 2\n" +
                    "3. Activity 3\n" +
                    "4. Exit");
                string userInput = Console.ReadLine();

                // Parse the userInput string into an ActivityType
                if(Enum.TryParse<ActivityType>(userInput, out ActivityType selectedActivity))
                {
                    switch(selectedActivity)
                    {
                        case ActivityType.Activity1:
                            PerformActivity1();
                            break;
                        case ActivityType.Activity2:
                            PerformActivity2();
                            break;
                        case ActivityType.Activity3:
                            PerformActivity3();
                            break;
                        case ActivityType.Exit:
                            continueApp = false; // Exit the app
                            break;
                    }
                }
                else
                {
                    Console.WriteLine("Invalid action, please choose again.");
                }
            }
        }
        
        // Create methods to handle each activity
        static void PerformActivity1()
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Performing Activity 1...");
            // Add your logic here
        }

        static void PerformActivity2()
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Performing Activity 2...");
            // Add your logic here
        }

        static void PerformActivity3()
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Performing Activity 3...");
            // Add your logic here
        }
    }
}
```

In this program, we have created--------------------        }
