In real tests, you should use PexInstrumentedSource and PexInstrumentationProvider to inject the Pex methods into your program. However, these classes aren't part of the .NET framework and are only available in the Pex plug-in for Visual Studio. To use Pex without the plug-in, all you need to do is add the [PexAssemblyUnderTest] attribute to the unit tests.

```Csharp
using System;
using System.Collections;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;

public static class PexTests
{
    [PexMethodUnderTest]
    [PexInstrumentedSource("PUT_SetContract")]
    public static void SetValueAndCheck(ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
    {
        int oldSize = arrList.Count;
        int oldFirstIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
        int oldLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
        bool oldContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int New_arrListFirstIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
        int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
        bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        // [PexAssumeUnderTest] here will force Pex to consider this method under test for test generation
        // This method and the PexAssumeUnderTest methods together form a complete test that Pex can generate
        Assume.Abs(index >=0 && index < arrList.Count);
        Assert.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);
    }
}

```

Remember to replace the placeholders with your own code in your unit tests. Also, remember to follow good coding practices to make your code as robust and easy to maintain. Happy Pexing!!!

