# Testing on a huge production codebase

There are several challenges when writing tests for a big production codebase. These can include managing dependencies, isolating individual unit tests, handling mocking frameworks, dealing with slow or flaky tests, and maintaining a large test suite.--------------------        }

    }
}
```

In the end we make use of `PexObserve.ValueForViewing` to record the old and new values of variables we care about, then use a debug assertion (`Debug.Assert`) to make sure our update didn't disrupt the order and presence of the element `x` in the list. 

This makes our test more complete, as it now not only validates the expected behavior (it should insert `x` at `index` in the array list), but also verifies its invariants (the Count, IndexOf, LastIndexOf and Contains methods' results before and after the operation should remain consistent).

## References

1. [Unit Testing with Invariants](https://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/people/simonpj/papers/invariants/Invariants.pdf) .pdf - A paper on the use of invariants in rigorous testing.

2. [Pex - The .NET Testing Tool for All .NET Developers](https://github.com/microsoft/Pex) .NET Testing framework that supports testing .NET code for: unit testing, state testing, property testing, path testing, fuzz testing, coverage testing, and equivalence testing. 

3. [Arrange Act Assert Pattern](https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/dd381645.aspx) - Use this pattern to structure your unit tests. This pattern encourages writing tests that are simple, easy to understand, and easy to maintain. 

3. [Arrange Act Assert](https://www.hanselman.com/blog/the-worlds-simplest-and-most-powerful-unit-tests-arrangeactassert/) - A best practice for unit testing that uses the 'Arrange-Act-Assert' (AAA) pattern to help ensure your unit tests are simple, maintainable and work as expected.

[category:Articles](category:Articles) [category:Unit_Testing](category:Unit_Testing) [category:Testing_frameworks](category:Testing_frameworks) [category:ArrayList](category:ArrayList) [category:C#](category:C#) 

```python
# Let's assume the following method is under test

public List<int> GetArray()
{
    List<int> list = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3, 4 };
    return list;
}
```

In order to write a unit test method for `GetArray`, `List<int>` should be mockable in order to validate the behavior of the method independently from its implementation. Luckily, in C# we have several frameworks for creating mocks and fakes. 

In the following test method, we use NUnit and MOQ framework. Assumed that `GetArray` method is not dependent on anything else but just returns a list. We want to test whether our method returns an instance of `List<int>`.

```python
[Test]
public void Test_GetArray()
{
    // Arrange
    var mockList = new Mock<List<int>>();

    // Act
    var actualResult = mockList.Object;

    // Assert
    Assert.IsInstanceOf<List<int>>(actualResult);
}
```

In the arrange step, a mock of `List<int>` is created using MOQ framework. By default, a mock object returns a default value for any property or method it doesn't explicitly set, so our `actualResult` should hold the default value for `List<int>`.

In the act step, we simply call the `Object` property of the mock, which gives us the concrete implementation of whatever we were expecting from our original `GetArray` method implementation.

In the assert step, we check whether or not our `actualResult` is an instance of `List<int>`.

This is a basic test which shows that mocking can provide a way to achieve unit testing on the return types. More complex test scenarios would involve verifying the behavior of the mock objects themselves - for example, by setting up a mock to throw an exception when a certain method is called, and then making sure the correct exception is thrown in the test.

Please note, mocking is a powerful tool when used correctly, but creating and maintaining mocks can become very complex when dealing with larger and more complicated applications. Testing without mock objects can also potentially lead to brittle unit tests.  Mockito, NUnit, MOQ are some popular mock--------------------            PexAssert.IsTrue(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  new arrList Count remains same 
            PexAssert.AreEqual(New_arrListCount, Old_arrListCount);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  last IndexOf x in old array is <= same in new array 
            PexAssert.IsTrue(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  new arrList contains x 
            PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  new IndexOf x in arrList <= old IndexOf x 
            PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
        }

        // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that last IndexOf x in old array <= its current last IndexOf in array 
        PexAssert.IsTrue(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);  
    }
}
```

In this example, assume we have an array list 'arrList' and 'x' and 'index' are also given. After setting the contract, the array list should maintain all its previous properties (like count, index of x, etc.), and also x should be its last occurrence in the arrayList.

**Note:** In C#, you can use the `Debug.Assert()` function to add an assertion. For example: `Debug.Assert(condition, "message");`. This is a useful feature when you want to stop program execution if the condition is not met. This will cause an error to be thrown in a Debugging environment, but not in a Release environment.

### PexAssumeUnderTest attribute

The `[PexAssumeUnderTest]` attribute is used to denote the test class or method under test. It indicates that the test method or type is a unit test and shouldn't be executed normally, but rather only when called via Test Explorer (or equivalent in your testing framework). The test method is run under the assumption that the method under test is correctly implemented and can be tested separately.

In the given example, the `PUT_SetContract` method represents the method we are testing which sets a contract at a particular index in our array list.

### PexObserve and PexAssert

`PexObserve.ValueForViewing(string name, int value)` method is used to observe a variable. The first parameter is the name of the variable and the second parameter is the value of the variable.

`PexAssert.IsTrue(bool condition)` method is used to validate a condition. If the condition is false, the test will fail.

`PexAssert.AreEqual(int expected, int actual)` method is used to check if two values are equal. If they are not equal, the test will fail.

These two methods are part of the Pex Framework and are used to verify the correctness of the method under test.

### PexConsider

The `PexConsider.ValueForViewing` method is similar to `PexObserve.ValueForViewing`, but it is used to show a different variable under different circumstances. It can be used to change the input parameters of the test method based on conditions. For example, we might want to consider different edge cases for a method under test.

## Conclusion

In the given example, the Pex Framework was used to perform white-box testing on an ArrayList class. It automatically generated test inputs, system states, and behaviors to create a comprehensive test suite. This example demonstrates how to use Pex Attributes and methods for different stages of testing like AssumeUnderTest, Consider, Observe, and Assert. It shows how to verify method contracts like the count of the ArrayList, the position of elements, whether the ArrayList contains the element, etc., and it can generate a large amount of test case combinations automatically, resulting in a more robust and extensive testing approach.

However, Pex testing tools are still quite experimental and are not yet included in standard programming environments. But with more and more companies moving towards more agile development methodologies, test automation tools are becoming more and more important, and Pex is no exception to this trend. So learn and master these tools, it will definitely help you in any software development scenarios--------------------